.TH E1000E 4 "2026-09-30" "Substrate Kernel" "Kernel Interfaces"
.SH NAME
e1000e \- Intel 82574 and I217/I218/I219 Gigabit Ethernet driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B device e1000e
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B e1000e
driver supports Intel's PCI Express Gigabit Ethernet controllers that
keep the 8254x register interface but need a different bring-up from the
parts
.B e1000
drives:
.TP
.B 82574L, 82574LA, 82583V
discrete controllers (8086:10D3, 10F6, 150C).  QEMU's
.B -device e1000e
is an 82574L.
.TP
.B I217, I218
the network MAC inside Lynx Point and Wildcat Point chipsets, with its
PHY on the chipset's MDIO bus.
.TP
.B I219
the same from Sunrise Point (6th-generation Core) onwards, through
I219-LM12/V12.
.PP
The interface is registered as
.BR eth0 .
One controller is supported; a second is reported and ignored.
.SS Data path
One receive and one transmit ring of legacy 16-byte descriptors, 32 and 16
entries, with 2 KiB buffers.  The Ethernet CRC is stripped on receive and
inserted on transmit.  Every supported part accepts legacy descriptors
while extended receive status is off, and the driver turns it off in case
a boot ROM left it on.
.PP
The station address is taken from Receive Address 0, which the hardware
loads from its NVM at reset.  The chipset parts keep that NVM in the
system's SPI flash rather than behind an EEPROM interface.  If the
reloaded address is invalid the one firmware left there is used; with
neither, the driver does not attach.
.SS Bring-up
At attach the driver stops receive and transmit, then resets the MAC.
On the chipset parts it resets the MAC only, never the PHY: the
Management Engine may own the PHY (shown in the log as
.RB \(lq "PHY reset blocked by firmware" \(rq),
and PHY reset has proven unreliable on these parts.  The MAC reset runs
under the software semaphore in
.B EXTCNF_CTRL
that arbitrates shared resources with firmware.
.PP
An I219 can hang in reset if descriptors were left pending, for example
after a PXE boot; the hardware reports this in PCI configuration space.
When it does, the driver first pushes one dummy transmit through its own
ring and, if that is not enough, cycles the receiver, as Linux does.
.PP
After reset the driver sets
.B CTRL_EXT.DRV_LOAD
so that manageability firmware (Intel AMT on vPro machines) hands the
port over, and leaves speed and duplex to autonegotiation.
.SS PHY
The PHY is reached through the MDIC register at MDIO address 1 (82574)
or 2 (chipset parts), under the same software semaphore.  Its ID and
control register are logged.  A PHY left powered down by a previous
operating system is powered up and autonegotiation restarted.
.PP
A PHY that does not answer is logged as
.RB \(lq "PHY not responding on MDIO (Ultra Low Power mode?)" \(rq .
I217 and later PHYs can be left in Ultra Low Power mode, which the driver
does not yet know how to leave; the link may still come up if firmware
brought the PHY out of it.
.SS Interrupts
The driver tries MSI first, then the firmware's legacy interrupt line,
then legacy INTx routed through the I/O APIC by the conventional PCI
interrupt swizzle.  Each source is proven by raising a link-status-change
cause through the Interrupt Cause Set register and waiting for it to
arrive; one that stays silent is removed and the next is tried.  If none
can be proven, the first that installs is kept with a warning.  On a
shared line the handler acts only when
.B ICR
reports that this device asserted it.
.PP
Link changes are logged with speed and duplex.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B sys/drivers/net/e1000e.c
The driver.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B "e1000e: <part> <device-id> <MAC> msi|irq <n>"
Attached; followed by the link state.
.TP
.B "e1000e: MSI <n> verified"
The interrupt source that passed the self-test.
.TP
.B "e1000e: WARNING: using unverified ..."
No source passed the self-test; receive may not work.
.TP
.B "e1000e: descriptor flush required before reset (I219)"
The I219 reset-hang workaround ran.
.SH BUGS
The older chipset PHYs \(em 82577 and 82578 (Ibex Peak) and 82579
(Cougar Point, Panther Point) \(em are not supported; they need
per-generation PHY workarounds.  Ultra Low Power exit is not implemented.
No jumbo frames, checksum offload, or segmentation offload.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ifconfig (8)
.SH HISTORY
Written for Substrate in 2026, following Linux's
.B e1000e
and iPXE's
.B intel
drivers.  Tested under QEMU (82574L); the I217 and I219 support awaits
real hardware.
